WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.877
You bring a careful recommendation into the room.

2
00:00:03.877 --> 00:00:05.815
The research is sound.

3
00:00:05.815 --> 00:00:10.661
The test plan has a measure and a stop condition.

4
00:00:10.661 --> 00:00:13.568
Someone asks about a competing priority.

5
00:00:13.568 --> 00:00:16.476
Engineering asks whose work will move.

6
00:00:16.476 --> 00:00:22.291
A person who was not in the meeting raises a concern afterward.

7
00:00:22.291 --> 00:00:22.920
The answer becomes, “Let's revisit this next quarter.” Chapter Fourteen opens with a version of that slow no.

8
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.373
Its lesson is practical: the evidence may be strong while the path to a decision is weak.

9
00:00:28.373 --> 00:00:34.561
People in the room have different information, responsibilities, and memories of the last failed bet.

10
00:00:34.561 --> 00:00:41.484
A proposal can be right about the customer and still arrive in the wrong form.

11
00:00:41.484 --> 00:00:43.792
The team sees the research.

12
00:00:43.792 --> 00:00:46.099
Finance sees an unpriced commitment.

13
00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:50.253
Engineering sees work that may displace a promised upgrade.

14
00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:57.174
The person presenting keeps adding evidence, but no one has named the decision that each listener is being asked to carry.

15
00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:02.962
It also returns to the decision theater: stakeholders have authority, fears, and limited attention.

16
00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.534
Our membership-service example is fictional.

17
00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:11.331
In Chapter Seventeen, Sam arrives at a roadmap meeting that feels different.

18
00:01:11.331 --> 00:01:19.061
The team has already gathered feedback, translated it into a question, and prepared a learning choice.

19
00:01:19.061 --> 00:01:23.408
The meeting can spend its time on the decision.

20
00:01:23.408 --> 00:01:27.014
Sam is a character in the book's story, not a member of a team we are showing live.

21
00:01:27.014 --> 00:01:28.814
Evidence had a place to arrive.

22
00:01:28.814 --> 00:01:30.324
A plan had a review point.

23
00:01:30.324 --> 00:01:34.446
A failed test could be discussed without scrambling to explain it away.

24
00:01:34.446 --> 00:01:39.846
Think about your last meeting that started with everyone retelling the same customer problem.

25
00:01:39.846 --> 00:01:42.945
The feedback pile is growing again.

26
00:01:42.945 --> 00:01:50.691
One note says, “I could not tell where to start.” Another asks for a reminder.

27
00:01:50.691 --> 00:01:57.405
A third says the task looked complete but the page gave no confirmation.

28
00:01:57.405 --> 00:02:03.085
These are fictional member comments carried forward from our earlier exercise.

29
00:02:03.085 --> 00:02:04.366
If we group them too quickly under “improve onboarding,” we lose the difference between finding an action, remembering it, and knowing it worked.

30
00:02:04.366 --> 00:02:09.285
Chapter Nineteen introduces ClientQ as a way to translate messy feedback into jobs.

31
00:02:09.285 --> 00:02:12.605
The book case gives us a method for examining the notes.

32
00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:14.547
Picture a Monday meeting.

33
00:02:14.547 --> 00:02:16.489
The dashboard is flat.

34
00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:26.684
Someone says the product needs a pivot, and the room starts naming new features before anyone has agreed what is stuck.

35
00:02:26.684 --> 00:02:31.539
In Chapter Twenty-One, Sam faces that pressure and opens StratQuest.

36
00:02:31.539 --> 00:02:33.966
This is a book story.

37
00:02:33.966 --> 00:02:36.392
Our membership-service example is a separate teaching exercise.

38
00:02:36.392 --> 00:02:38.595
Start with four possible constraints.

39
00:02:38.595 --> 00:02:40.857
Is the customer job poorly understood?

40
00:02:40.857 --> 00:02:42.465
Is the product hard to use?

41
00:02:42.465 --> 00:02:46.156
Does the way people pay conflict with when they receive value?

42
00:02:46.156 --> 00:02:48.020
The plan is ready.

43
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.349
The evidence ledger is organized.

44
00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:58.270
Yet the first person hearing the recommendation asks about delivery cost, while the second asks who benefits.

45
00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:04.326
Chapter Twenty-Three calls this the last-mile problem and introduces Persuasive Business Case GPT.

46
00:03:04.326 --> 00:03:08.986
That is a book case, not a live demonstration here.

47
00:03:08.986 --> 00:03:12.436
Return to the membership-service alternative from Lesson Thirteen.

48
00:03:12.436 --> 00:03:14.646
One decision owner manages operations.

49
00:03:14.646 --> 00:03:17.554
Another represents the people who use the service.

50
00:03:17.554 --> 00:03:19.772
One small test was useful.

51
00:03:19.772 --> 00:03:23.321
A second team asks to run it too.

52
00:03:23.321 --> 00:03:26.870
That sounds like progress, and it may be.

53
00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.419
It also changes the shape of the work.

54
00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:38.405
The first team knew the people, the context, and the reason for every field in its learning plan.

55
00:03:38.405 --> 00:03:40.061
The second team receives a template and a sentence about success.

56
00:03:40.061 --> 00:03:49.600
Chapter Twenty-Four asks what changes at scale: experiments can collide, metrics can mean different things, and a tool can spread faster than a review practice.

57
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.390
Go back to the decision you wrote on the first page.

58
00:03:54.390 --> 00:04:03.100
At the time, it may have been a broad request: improve onboarding, grow revenue, or use AI to move faster.

59
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.148
The file now gives that request edges.

60
00:04:06.148 --> 00:04:14.500
It names the person served, the evidence that exists, the alternatives considered, the test that could change the choice, and the people who must review it.

61
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.934
That is the right size for the next week.

62
00:04:16.934 --> 00:04:22.633
Look at the Signals, Alignment, and Momentum labels on the approved Accelerated SAM infographic.
